Pipnode runtime dependencies
============================

The rest of this file lists optional runtime data (fonts, TTS voices).
For the GUI vs. headless split and how to package it, see "Runtime tiers
and packaging" at the end.

Node icons are drawn with Pango and rely on fontconfig finding the
FontAwesome face -- glyphs in the U+F000..U+F8FF Private Use Area
have no fallback in standard system fonts and would otherwise render
as tofu.  On Debian/Ubuntu install:

    sudo apt install fonts-font-awesome

This ships /usr/share/fonts/truetype/font-awesome/fontawesome-webfont.ttf
under the family name "FontAwesome" (the v4 webfont), which is what
the worksheet's Pango font description matches against.

The text-to-speech (TTS) sink node shells out to two external tools:

  * piper  -- neural TTS engine; not packaged in standard Ubuntu
              repositories, install from upstream releases.
  * aplay  -- ALSA playback front-end; ships with the alsa-utils
              package on Debian/Ubuntu (`sudo apt install alsa-utils`).


Installing Piper
----------------

1. Grab the latest pre-built release for your architecture from
       https://github.com/rhasspy/piper/releases
   The Linux build is shipped as `piper_linux_<arch>.tar.gz` and
   bundles its own libonnxruntime, libespeak-ng and voice phonemiser
   data; nothing else needs to be on the system.

2. Extract the bundle somewhere stable -- the binary uses an
   $ORIGIN-relative RUNPATH, so the surrounding files must travel
   with it:

       mkdir -p ~/.local/share
       tar -xzf piper_linux_x86_64.tar.gz -C ~/.local/share
       # this creates ~/.local/share/piper/

3. Put the launcher on PATH:

       ln -s ~/.local/share/piper/piper ~/.local/bin/piper

   Make sure ~/.local/bin is in $PATH (it is by default on most
   Ubuntu installs).


Installing a voice
------------------

Piper voices are downloaded separately from the Hugging Face
collection at https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices.  The TTS
node defaults to the Lessac English voice; install it with:

    mkdir -p ~/.local/share/piper/voices
    cd ~/.local/share/piper/voices
    base=https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/resolve/main/en/en_US/lessac/medium
    curl -fsSL -O $base/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx
    curl -fsSL -O $base/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx.json

Both files (the .onnx model and its .onnx.json sidecar) must sit next
to each other.  Other voices follow the same layout under their own
language/speaker/quality directory in the upstream repo.


Adding more voices
------------------

Every .onnx file present in ~/.local/share/piper/voices/ shows up in
the TTS node's model dropdown automatically (labelled by its
"speaker-quality" suffix), so installing a new voice is just a matter
of dropping its two files in beside the existing ones.  The four
English voices Pipnode is most often demoed with are:

    base=https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/resolve/main/en/en_US
    cd ~/.local/share/piper/voices
    for path in amy/medium/en_US-amy-medium \
                lessac/medium/en_US-lessac-medium \
                libritts_r/medium/en_US-libritts_r-medium \
                ryan/medium/en_US-ryan-medium; do
        curl -fsSL -O "$base/$path.onnx"
        curl -fsSL -O "$base/$path.onnx.json"
    done

Voices for other languages live under sibling locale directories
(e.g. de/de_DE, fr/fr_FR, ...) using the same naming scheme.  Browse
https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/tree/main for the full
catalogue.


Verifying
---------

A round-trip smoke test:

    echo "Pipnode is talking." \
        | piper -m ~/.local/share/piper/voices/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx -q -f - \
        | aplay -q -

If you hear the sentence, the TTS node is ready to use.


Runtime tiers and packaging
===========================

Pipnode separates its GUI from its flow engine at runtime, so the same
build serves both a desktop install and a headless server.

Two binaries:

  * pipnode-editor -- the GTK application (the visual editor).
  * pipnode-run    -- runs a saved worksheet with NO GUI: load JSON,
                      run the flow, emit messages.  Debug-node output
                      goes to stdout, status to stderr.  Useful under
                      cron/systemd on a machine with no display.

Two libraries:

  * libpipnode-core -- GTK-free.  The flow engine, the message bus, the
                       node factory, and ALL node logic (receive()).
                       Depends only on GLib/GObject/GIO, JSON-GLib,
                       libsoup, GModule and (for image data) gdk-pixbuf.
  * libpipnode-gui  -- links core and adds the editor, the canvas
                       renderer, every node's cairo drawing, the settings
                       dialogs, GtkSourceView and (optional) WebKit.
                       This is the tier that needs GTK, cairo, pango,
                       PLplot, GtkSourceView.

pipnode-run links libpipnode-core ALONE; verify with:

    objdump -p $(command -v pipnode-run) | grep NEEDED

It lists no libgtk/libgdk/cairo/pango.  So a server can execute
worksheets without the GTK stack installed at all.  (The split is a
runtime + packaging property: the project is always BUILT on a host that
has the full GTK stack -- there is no --disable-gui build switch.)

Splitting into distribution packages
------------------------------------

One GTK-equipped build can be cut into:

  * pipnode-core  -- pipnode-run, libpipnode-core, and the core-only
                     bundled plugins (network, image, Tasmota, plus the
                     logic .so of any two-tier plugin).  Dependencies:
                     GLib, JSON-GLib, libsoup, gdk-pixbuf, libmosquitto,
                     GnuTLS.  NO GTK.  This is all a headless server needs.
  * pipnode-gui   -- pipnode-editor, libpipnode-gui, and the companion
                     "<plugin>-gui.so" modules.  Depends on pipnode-core
                     plus GTK 3, GtkSourceView, PLplot and (optionally)
                     WebKit2GTK.  Pulls in fonts-font-awesome for the
                     node glyphs.

Bundled plugins follow the same rule.  A plugin is either:

  * core-only: a single GTK-free <plugin>.so that links libpipnode-core
    (network, image, tasmota) -- belongs in pipnode-core; or
  * two-tier: a GTK-free <plugin>.so PLUS an editor-only <plugin>-gui.so
    companion (shell, sound-effects) -- the .so belongs in pipnode-core,
    the -gui.so in pipnode-gui.

The core loader scans the plugin directory for <plugin>.so and skips any
"<plugin>-gui.so"; the editor additionally loads the matching companion.
So a pipnode-core-only install simply has no -gui.so files present, and
every node still runs (it just shows the host's default dialog if its
custom dialog lived in the companion).  See PLUGINS for the full plugin
model and a checklist for shipping a server-installable plugin.


Uninstalling
============

Automake generates a "make uninstall" target, so removing an installed
build is the inverse of installing it.  Run it from the SAME build tree
you installed from -- it deletes the files that tree's "make install"
wrote -- with the same privileges and the same --prefix:

    sudo make uninstall

It recurses through every subdirectory and removes the binaries
(pipnode-editor, pipnode-run), the libpipnode-core / libpipnode-gui
libraries, the bundled plugins, the help pages and icons under
$pkgdatadir, the pkg-config and aclocal developer files, and every
bundled example worksheet under $pkgdatadir/examples/<category>/.

As is standard for Automake, "make uninstall" removes files but not the
directories it created, so empty directories such as
$pkgdatadir/examples/ are left behind.  To prune the example tree too
($pkgdatadir defaults to /usr/local/share/pipnode):

    sudo make uninstall
    sudo rmdir -p /usr/local/share/pipnode/examples/* 2>/dev/null

It does not touch anything installed outside this build tree -- e.g. a
stale plugin you moved aside into plugins.disabled/, or an out-of-tree
plugin installed by its own "make install".
